---
// SEO article for the RPE chart. The tables are built from the same data as the chart.
import { defaultPercent, WHOLE_RPES } from '../lib/rpe.js';
const compare = [[5, 8], [8, 8], [10, 8], [12, 10], [12, 8], [12, 6]];
const rows = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12];
---
<section aria-labelledby="about-tool" class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 pt-16">
  <article class="prose-site">
    <h2 id="about-tool" style="margin-top:0">What an RPE chart shows</h2>
    <p>RPE means rate of perceived exertion. In lifting, it is a score from 1 to 10 for how hard a set felt. An RPE of 10 is a set with nothing left. An RPE of 8 is a set where you could have done about 2 more reps. RIR means reps in reserve. It is the same idea, counted the other way: RPE 8 equals 2 RIR.</p>
    <p>An RPE chart turns that score into a share of your one-rep max (1RM). Find your reps and your RPE, and the chart gives a percentage. Many lifters use it to pick the weight for the day. On a strong day the bar moves fast, so they go up. On a bad day they stay put and still hit the planned RPE.</p>

    <h2>How to use the RPE calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Pick pounds or kilograms, and pick RPE or RIR.</li>
      <li>Enter the weight of a hard set you did, the reps you did, and how hard it felt.</li>
      <li>Read your estimated 1RM. It is the weight divided by the chart percentage.</li>
      <li>Under "Plan your next set", pick the reps and RPE you want. The tool shows the load for that set.</li>
      <li>Choose the smallest jump your plates can make. The load rounds to it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>You squat 225 lb for 5 reps, and it felt like RPE 8. The chart shows 81.1 percent for 5 reps at RPE 8. Your estimated 1RM is 225 &divide; 0.811 = 277.4 lb.</p>
    <p>Next you want 3 reps at RPE 8. The chart shows 86.3 percent. That is 277.4 &times; 0.863 = 239.4 lb. With 5 lb jumps, you load 240 lb.</p>

    <h2>RPE chart for whole RPE values</h2>
    <p>These are the values of the current RTS table (the default version) for whole RPE numbers. The full chart above also has half steps and 7, 9 and 11 reps, and the 2016 version.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Reps</th>{WHOLE_RPES.map((r) => <th scope="col">RPE {r}</th>)}</tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((reps) => (
            <tr><td>{reps}</td>{WHOLE_RPES.map((r) => <td>{defaultPercent(reps, r, 'current').toFixed(1)}%</td>)}</tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>RPE and RIR scale</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">RPE</th><th scope="col">RIR</th><th scope="col">What it means</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>10</td><td>0</td><td>Maximum effort. No reps left.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>9.5</td><td>0.5</td><td>No reps left, but you could add a little weight.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>9</td><td>1</td><td>One rep left.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8</td><td>2</td><td>Two reps left.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7</td><td>3</td><td>Three reps left. The bar still moves fast.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6</td><td>4</td><td>Four or more reps left. A light set.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Two chart versions</h2>
    <p>Reactive Training Systems (RTS, Mike Tuchscherer) has published more than one RPE chart, and the numbers are not the same. This page has both. A switch in the calculator picks one.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Set</th><th scope="col">Current RTS (default)</th><th scope="col">2016 RTS article</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {compare.map(([reps, rpe]) => (
            <tr><td>{reps} {reps === 1 ? 'rep' : 'reps'} at RPE {rpe}</td><td>{defaultPercent(reps, rpe, 'current').toFixed(1)}%</td><td>{defaultPercent(reps, rpe, 'y2016').toFixed(1)}%</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The versions differ by up to 7.6 points at high reps. They agree within 0.1 point when reps plus reps in reserve is 10 or less, so low-rep sets give the same answer in both.</p>

    <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
    <p><strong>Current RTS (default).</strong> The current RTS table from their TRAC system. We read it from the open data file <code>rpe_tab_rts.csv</code> in the R package <code>strength</code>. Its documentation says the table was pulled from TRAC in December 2025. We did not read it on the RTS site itself.</p>
    <p><strong>2016 RTS article.</strong> The chart from the RTS article "Customizing Your RPE Chart" (January 6, 2016), as copied in the open-source RPECalc app. That table ends at RPE 6.5. We add the RPE 6 row with the reps plus RIR rule.</p>
    <p>Both charts follow one rule: reps plus reps in reserve decides the percentage. Five reps at RPE 8 leave 2 in reserve, so they use the same percentage as 7 reps at RPE 10. The RPE 6 values of the 2016 version are extended this way, because that article has no RPE 6 row.</p>
    <p>The RPE scale based on reps in reserve comes from Zourdos and colleagues (2016, <em>Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research</em>), who tested it on the squat. Helms and colleagues (2016, <em>Strength and Conditioning Journal</em>) explained how to apply it in training.</p>

    <h2>Limits you should know</h2>
    <p>The chart gives an estimate, not a test. People rate effort in different ways. New lifters often rate a set as easier or harder than it was. Ratings tend to be more accurate on heavy sets close to failure, and they improve with practice. Above about 10 reps, expect a rough estimate: it is harder to judge how many reps are left in a long set, and the two chart versions differ most there. Edit any value to match the chart your coach uses.</p>
    <p>This tool is not medical advice. If you have a health condition or an injury, ask a doctor or a qualified coach before you train hard.</p>

    <h2>Why this RPE calculator</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It answers as you type. There is no button to press.</li>
      <li>You can edit every value in the chart.</li>
      <li>It has the current RTS table and the 2016 RTS chart, and it names each source.</li>
      <li>It rounds each load to your plates, in lb or kg, down to 1.25 lb and 0.5 kg.</li>
      <li>It works one-handed on a phone, with large buttons, and has a dark mode.</li>
      <li>It runs in your browser. It does not send what you type.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</section>

<style is:global>
  .prose-site .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 0 0 1.25rem; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: .75rem; max-width: 36rem; }
  .prose-site table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .975rem; }
  .prose-site th, .prose-site td { text-align: left; padding: .5rem .85rem; }
  .prose-site thead th { background: var(--brand-soft); color: var(--brand-ink); font-size: .8rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em; white-space: nowrap; }
  .prose-site tbody tr + tr td { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .prose-site td:first-child { font-weight: 650; white-space: nowrap; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
</style>
